{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-non-commutative-formal-group","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:non-commutative-formal-group","kind":"definition","title":"Non-commutative formal group and its distribution algebra","uses":["papers/fred2005-formal-groups/blocks/def-commutative-formal-group"],"cites":["references/leitner2006"],"tags":["formal-group","non-commutative","distribution-algebra"],"leanRef":"fred2005:Fred2005.NoncommutativeFormalGroup","text":"non-commutative-formal-group.md","leanSource":"non-commutative-formal-group.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:non-commutative-formal-group\"></a><p>A <strong>non-commutative formal group</strong> is a formal group that is not\nrequired to be commutative: an <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n</code>-dimensional formal group law <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">F</code> over\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">R</code> with no condition <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">F(X, Y) = F(Y, X)</code>. Every\n<a href=\"#def:commutative-formal-group\" data-at=\"formal-groups-chapter/commutative-formal-group\">commutative formal group</a> is one.</p>\n<p>Its <strong>distribution algebra</strong> <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(G)</code> is the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">R</code>-module of\ndistributions supported at the identity, the continuous dual of the\ncoordinate ring <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">R[[X_1, \\dots, X_n]]</code>. Dualising <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">F</code> gives the\nmultiplication (convolution) on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(G)</code>, and dualising the\nproduct of power series gives the comultiplication <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\Delta</code>, so\n<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(G)</code> is a bialgebra. It is commutative exactly when <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">G</code> is.</p>\n<p>Over a field <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code> of characteristic <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">p > 0</code>, with an ordered coordinate\nsystem <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">x_1 &#x3C; \\dots &#x3C; x_n</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Dist}(G)</code> has the <em>multiplicative\nbasis</em> of ordered products of the divided powers <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\delta_{x_j}^{p^r}</code>\n(<span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, Lemma/Definition 2.2).</p>\n<p>Reference: <span class=\"cite\" data-keys=\"leitner2006\">[leitner2006]</span>, §2.</p>\n","source":"folio/fred2005-formal-groups/formal-groups-chapter/non-commutative-formal-group.md","heading":"Definition","number":"1.1.2","page":"b/def-non-commutative-formal-group","qa":{"counts":{"pass":68,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":18,"unknown":0,"stale":44}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"fred2005:Fred2005.NoncommutativeFormalGroup","via":"sibling","path":"folio/fred2005-formal-groups/formal-groups-chapter/non-commutative-formal-group.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
